  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nMain forecast concern in the short term is severe storm \npotential as a dryline and cold front push in from the northwest\nthis evening. Early afternoon analysis showed a surface low \nspinning over central SD with the dryline stretching south- \nsouthwest into the NE panhandle and cold front west toward the \nBlack Hills. Closer to home and ahead of these features surface \ndewpoints had climbed into the mid to upper 60s, with a few \nspotty 70 degree readings. However, 18Z OAX sounding showed \nquite a bit of dry air aloft, though farther northwest, strong \nlow-level moisture transport was taking place, so said dry air \nshouldn't be too much of a hindrance to convective initiation. \nHowever, the strongest forcing for ascent will remain tied \ncloser to the surface low and shortwave as they advance more \nnortheastward into MN. Plus, it looks like we'll have at least \nsome degree of capping in place which may somewhat limit storm \ndevelopment. All that said, latest short term guidance is \ntrending toward a little bit more coverage in storms as the cold\nfront impinges on the dryline in northeast NE this evening and \npushes south into the overnight. With guidance suggesting 3000+ \nJ/kg of MUCAPE and maybe a little bit stronger deep layer shear \nthan previous solutions (35-40 kts in the 0-6 km layer), if \nstorms do form, they could be a little more likely to produce \nsevere weather. Still, think the aforementioned capping will \nlimit things a bit, with less of that shear being \"available\" \nfor any given storm, so storms may be a bit disorganized. So \nbottom line, think we'll see a few storms go up along the \nboundary in northeast NE/west-central IA this evening and \nquickly rain/hail themselves out, but not before producing some \n50-60+ mph winds and perhaps some large hail. As outflow \ncontinues southeastward, additional storms may form into \nsoutheast NE/southwest IA, but they should be weaker. Currently \nlooking at a 20-40% chance of storms with a 5-15% chance of a \nsevere storm near a given point.\n\nHeading into Saturday morning, the front should be just south \nof the NE/KS border while some weak shortwave energy currently \nin the UT/ID/WY border area pushes through and lingering \nmoisture transport points toward the forecast area. As a result,\nwe could see additional spotty showers and a few storms through\nthe morning on Saturday, mainly across southeast NE/southwest \nIA, where there's a 20-30% chance. The boundary should remain \nstrewn across northern KS into Saturday evening while moisture \ntransport ramps back up and points toward the forecast area, \nbringing yet another round of potential showers and storms. \nWhile much of the instability will remain south of the boundary,\nguidance suggests we'll have at least a little elevated \ninstability in our area, along with pretty good deep layer \nshear. Once again, could see a few stronger to isolated severe \nstorms Saturday evening into the overnight (30-40% chance).\n\nThe train of shortwaves will continue Sunday into next week \nwhile the surface boundary to our south will lift northward \nSunday night into Monday bringing continued shower and storm \nchances. There will be potential for severe storms at times, \nespecially Monday as the boundary sets up over us and we start \nto see a little bit stronger upper level flow nudge into the \narea. That said, with overnight and morning storm chances, \ndestabilization will be a question mark, so still lots of \ndetails to work out there. Overall, it's looking like a pretty \nwet work week with that boundary remaining in our vicinity and \nvarious shortwaves pushing through. Chances currently top out in\nthe 70-90% range Monday and Tuesday with continued 30-40+ \npercent chances into at least Friday. Given the potential for \nrepeated rounds of storms and an environment conducive to \nefficient rain-producing storms, potential for localized \nflooding will increase with each successive round. Otherwise, \ngiven the boundary and precip chances, temperature forecast will\nbe a bit tricky, but in general, expect a mild weekend with \nhighs in the upper 60s and 70s, a warmer Monday (upper 70s to \naround 90), followed by mostly 60s and 70s through the rest of \nthe week.",
